A||N||8||FieroJoe||Hood not laying flat, what to do?||||4||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||04-17-2001||10:45 AM||||Well, ever since I put my hood back on before we painted it, the left front corner does not sit flush with the fascia and fender when it is closed.  I dont remember it doing that before, I could have sworn that it sat like it supposed to before I took it off.  But then again, the last time that I looked at it assembled was in october.<P>So, my dad and I messed around with it, trying to get it to sit nicely all over.  One thing that we did not try was to leave the right hinge fully tightend down, loosen up the left hand one, and kinda of torque the hood down and then tighten the hinge back up.  Could this work?  In essence, I would be giving the hood a little more curvature, so maybe that corner will sit right.<P>Any other suggestions?<P>TIA<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Jay||04-17-2001||12:12 PM||||What I do to allign the hood is first take off the headlight covers.  This will give you a way into the hood compartment while the hood is down.  Adjust the hinge accordingly.  There are slots so that you can move the hood up or down.  When the correct height is obtained tighten the bolt.  Lift slowely to make sure that the front of the hood doesn't touch the bumber on it's way up.  Then replace the headlight covers.<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||daved||04-17-2001||12:16 PM||||Did you try adjusting the rubber bumper just in front of the windshield?  They just screw up and down.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||04-17-2001||12:37 PM||||both hinges are slotted so you can move them up or down and back and forth. just loosen bolts enough to let you bump it to change position till its where you want. its all trial and error.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-17-2001||07:49 PM||||I know it sounds obvious... Are you totally sure that corner isn't hitting something?||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||04-17-2001||08:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>I know it sounds obvious... Are you totally sure that corner isn't hitting something?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yeah, there are little black stops that can be adjusted up and down by turning them.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||04-17-2001||08:56 PM||||Thanks for all the replies.<P>As for the bumpers, it is not them causing the hood not to go down.  The bumpers arent even hitting where they are supposed to, I can just push down on the corner of the hood to make it lay like it should.<P>We tried and tried messing around with those hinges.  It still fits the best in the position that it was in before I took it off.  I aligned the nuts with the scratches in the paint that they had made when they were originally put on.<P>So I guess like rogergarrison said, its all just trial and error.  thanks again.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||04-17-2001||09:17 PM||||try moving the facia & fender up a bit and snug her back down<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bob Englert||04-17-2001||10:23 PM||||I had the same problem on my blue GT.  <P>Moving the slotted lower hinge didn't help me get it right.  On my car the previous owner had loosened the upper bolts that secure the hinge to the hood.  I loosened these and pushed the hood toward the hinge and tightened them back up at the hood sat much better than before<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
